IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

§ `British Nationality Act 1981, section 1 — Acquisition by birth or adoption`

-- The Home Office's clock: births, abandonments, adoption orders and
-- registration applications are dated as United Kingdom civil dates.
TIMEZONE IS "Europe/London"

-- ===========================================================================
-- BRITISH NATIONALITY ACT 1981 (c. 61), SECTION 1, as in force on 2026-07-04.
--
-- SCOPE. This module encodes section 1 only — acquisition of British
-- citizenship by birth or adoption — in its CURRENT consolidated text.
-- Subsections (1), (1A), (2), (3), (3A), (4), (5), (5A), (6) and (7) are
-- encoded as predicates. Subsection (8) is a definitional pointer to s 50
-- ("settled") and is carried by the @ref on the settled-parent input field
-- rather than expanded: the s 50(2)-(5) construction of "settled" (ordinary
-- residence, immigration-law restrictions, the s 50(4) exemption carve-back
-- and its diplomatic-immunity exception) is OUT OF SCOPE — settled-ness at
-- the time of the birth arrives as an already-construed input fact.
-- Subsection (9) is encoded as the dated constant `the relevant day`.
-- The REGISTRATION PROCESS (application form, fee, ceremony, s 41A grant
-- machinery) is out of scope; subsections (3), (3A) and (4) are encoded as
-- BOOLEAN entitlement predicates — "is entitled to be registered" — with the
-- s 41A good-character restriction (modification C3) as a separate
-- grant-gate predicate, not folded into the s 1 entitlements.
-- The historic pre-2002 / pre-2010 / pre-2021 texts are NOT encoded: no
-- rule-version axis in this smoke test. The HFEA parental-order
-- modifications (C1/C4/C5) are out of scope; so is modification C2
-- (S.I. 1972/1613 art. 6, as inserted by S.I. 1982/1649 art. 3), which
-- explains s 1(1) by treating certain persons exempt from immigration
-- control as settled — its substance rides inside the settled-as-input
-- scope-out above.
--
-- SOURCES. Every rule text quoted inline is verbatim from the
-- legislation.gov.uk revised text of s 1 as retrieved 2026-08-02 (snapshot
-- banner: "up to date with all changes known to be in force on or before
-- 04 July 2026"), saved with full provenance at source-s1.txt in this
-- directory. The [F-number] amendment markers of the source are dropped
-- from the inline quotes; the words are kept as they read in force.
-- Definitions come from s 50 (Annex A of source-s1.txt).
--
-- DATES AND THEIR CLOCKS. Three statutory days matter, each bound once
-- below: commencement (1 January 1983, s 50(1)), the appointed day
-- (21 May 2002, S.I. 2002/1252 art. 2) and the relevant day
-- (13 January 2010, s 1(9) with S.I. 2009/2731 art. 4(a)).
--
-- MODELLING NOTE. Constitutive material (who is, or is entitled to be
-- registered as, a British citizen) is encoded as `DECIDE`/`MEANS`
-- predicates carrying the Act's own words in the FIELD NAMES, with the
-- subsection or paragraph label riding beside its node as a truncated inert
-- string: `"(a)" ... person's `father or mother a British citizen at the time
-- of the birth``.
--
-- Under the enumeration-label ruling (2026-08-03) an inert string that merely
-- restates the node beside it is deleted -- inert never shadows active -- so a
-- verbatim quotation survives ONLY where no active node carries its words:
-- s 1(2)(a)-(b), whose facts are DEEMED rather than tested, and the s 1(5A)
-- chapeau, whose date framing the two limbs below it do not repeat. Three
-- mid-sentence fragments with no label of their own (in s 1(4) twice and in
-- the s 1(5) tail) are gone outright. Section
-- 1 imposes no obligations on anyone, so there is no regulative layer.
-- Facts about a person are threaded as one GIVEN PersonProfile record;
-- facts about an adoption as one GIVEN AdoptionCase record. Out-of-scope
-- statutory concepts (settled, member of the armed forces, section 10A
-- citizenship, good character) are record fields with @ref pointers to the
-- provisions that define them — inputs, not stubs.
-- ===========================================================================

§§ `Interpretation — the statutory days (s 50(1), s 1(9))`

-- AMBIGUITY: "after commencement" (ss (1), (2), (3), (4)). Commencement is
-- an instant — the Act commenced at the first moment of 1 January 1983 — so
-- a person born ON 1 January 1983 is born after commencement. Reading (i):
-- birth date >= 1983-01-01 (taken here, and the settled understanding).
-- Reading (ii): strictly after the whole commencement day, i.e. >=
-- 1983-01-02 (rejected: it would leave commencement-day births governed by
-- neither the old law nor the new). Encoded as AT LEAST 1983-01-01.

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 50(1) — "commencement", without more, means the commencement of this Act: 1 January 1983
GIVETH A DATE
`commencement` MEANS YMD 1983 1 1

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 50(1) — "appointed day"; S.I. 2002/1252 art. 2: 21 May 2002
GIVETH A DATE
`the appointed day` MEANS YMD 2002 5 21

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(9); S.I. 2009/2731 art. 4(a) — commencement of BCIA 2009 s 42: 13 January 2010
-- s 1(9): "The relevant day for the purposes of subsection (1A) or (3A) is
-- the day appointed for the commencement of section 42 of the Borders,
-- Citizenship and Immigration Act 2009 (which inserted those subsections)."
-- That day is fixed and resolvable at encode time, so it is bound here as a
-- constant — this IS the encoding of subsection (9).
GIVETH A DATE
`the relevant day` MEANS YMD 2010 1 13

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(4) — the ninety-day absence ceiling
GIVETH A NUMBER
`permitted days of absence in each of the first ten years` MEANS 90

§§ `The person — one record of birth, parentage and later-life facts`

-- NOTE on "the United Kingdom": by s 50(1) it "means Great Britain,
-- Northern Ireland and the Islands, taken together", where "the Islands"
-- are the Channel Islands and the Isle of Man. The `born in the United
-- Kingdom` and absence fields below carry that WIDER meaning — a birth in
-- Jersey or the Isle of Man is a birth in the United Kingdom for this Act.
--
-- Out-of-scope concepts arriving as inputs, with the provisions that
-- construe them:
--   settled                  s 50(2)-(5) (ordinary residence free of
--                            immigration-law time restriction; s 50(4)
--                            deems certain exempt parents settled for
--                            s 1(1) SPECIFICALLY, subject to a
--                            diplomatic-immunity exception; s 50(3)/(5)
--                            subtract exempt and unlawfully-present cases)
--   member of the armed forces  s 50(1A)-(1B) (regular or reserve forces
--                            under the Armed Forces Act 2006, minus the
--                            s 369 / Visiting Forces Act 1933 deemed cases)
--   British citizen under section 10A  s 10A (EU withdrawal agreement
--                            registration route, S.I. 2021/743)
--   good character           s 41A, applied to s 1(3)/(3A)/(4) applicants
--                            aged 10 or over by IANA 2006 s 58
--                            (modification C3)
@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1; s 50
DECLARE PersonProfile HAS
    name                                              IS A STRING
    `born in the United Kingdom`                      IS A BOOLEAN
    `born in a qualifying territory`                  IS A BOOLEAN
    `date of birth`                                   IS A DATE
    `father or mother a British citizen at the time of the birth`                                          IS A BOOLEAN
    `father or mother settled in the United Kingdom or the territory of birth at the time of the birth`    IS A BOOLEAN
    `father or mother a member of the armed forces at the time of the birth`                               IS A BOOLEAN
    `found abandoned as a new-born infant in the United Kingdom`                                           IS A BOOLEAN
    `found abandoned as a new-born infant in a qualifying territory`                                       IS A BOOLEAN
    `date found abandoned`                            IS A DATE
    `the contrary shown, rebutting the subsection (2) presumption`                                         IS A BOOLEAN
    `a British citizen under section 10A`             IS A BOOLEAN
    `while a minor, father or mother became a British citizen or became settled in the United Kingdom`     IS A BOOLEAN
    `while a minor, father or mother became a member of the armed forces`                                  IS A BOOLEAN
    `while a minor, an application made for registration as a British citizen`                             IS A BOOLEAN
    `an application for registration made after attaining the age of ten years`                            IS A BOOLEAN
    `greatest number of days absent from the United Kingdom in any one of the first ten years of life`     IS A NUMBER
    `the Secretary of State treats the subsection (4) residence requirement as fulfilled`                  IS A BOOLEAN
    `of good character, where the good character requirement applies`                                      IS A BOOLEAN

§§ `Common timing predicates`

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(1); s 50(1) "commencement"
GIVEN person IS A PersonProfile
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`born in the United Kingdom after commencement` person MEANS
        person's `born in the United Kingdom`
    AND person's `date of birth` AT LEAST `commencement`

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(1); s 50(1) "appointed day"
GIVEN person IS A PersonProfile
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`born in a qualifying territory on or after the appointed day` person MEANS
        person's `born in a qualifying territory`
    AND person's `date of birth` AT LEAST `the appointed day`

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(1A)
GIVEN person IS A PersonProfile
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`born in the United Kingdom or a qualifying territory` person MEANS
        person's `born in the United Kingdom`
    OR  person's `born in a qualifying territory`

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(3A)
GIVEN person IS A PersonProfile
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`born in the United Kingdom on or after the relevant day` person MEANS
        person's `born in the United Kingdom`
    AND person's `date of birth` AT LEAST `the relevant day`

§§ `Subsection (1) — birth in the United Kingdom or a qualifying territory`

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(1) chapeau — the birth limb
GIVEN person IS A PersonProfile
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`1(1) — born in the United Kingdom after commencement or in a qualifying territory on or after the appointed day` person MEANS
        `born in the United Kingdom after commencement` person
    OR  `born in a qualifying territory on or after the appointed day` person

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(1)(a)
GIVEN person IS A PersonProfile
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`1(1)(a) — father or mother a British citizen` person MEANS
        "(a)" ... person's `father or mother a British citizen at the time of the birth`

-- AMBIGUITY: "(b) settled in the United Kingdom or that territory" — "that
-- territory" refers back to the particular qualifying territory of the
-- birth, not to qualifying territories at large. Reading (i): the parent
-- must be settled in the UK or in the SAME territory where the birth
-- occurred (taken here — the anaphor resolves to the birth territory, and
-- the input field is worded "or the territory of birth" to put that burden
-- on the fact-supplier). Reading (ii): settled in the UK or in ANY
-- qualifying territory (rejected: "that" is a bound anaphor, not "a").
@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(1)(b); s 50(2)-(5) construe "settled"
GIVEN person IS A PersonProfile
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`1(1)(b) — father or mother settled in the United Kingdom or that territory` person MEANS
        "(b)" ... person's `father or mother settled in the United Kingdom or the territory of birth at the time of the birth`

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(1)(a)-(b) — the parent-status disjunction
GIVEN person IS A PersonProfile
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`1(1) — at the time of the birth his father or mother is a citizen or settled` person MEANS
        `1(1)(a) — father or mother a British citizen` person
    ..  `1(1)(b) — father or mother settled in the United Kingdom or that territory` person

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(1)
GIVEN person IS A PersonProfile
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`1(1) — satisfied on the actual facts of the birth` person MEANS
        "(1)" ... `1(1) — born in the United Kingdom after commencement or in a qualifying territory on or after the appointed day` person
    AND `1(1) — at the time of the birth his father or mother is a citizen or settled` person

§§ `Subsection (2) — the foundling presumption`

-- AMBIGUITY: "new-born infant" is undefined by the Act. Reading (i): an
-- infant abandoned within days or weeks of birth, as assessed by the
-- decision-maker (taken here — the boolean input field asserts
-- new-born-ness as a found fact, leaving the vague boundary to the
-- fact-supplier). Reading (ii): any abandoned child of apparently
-- qualifying age (rejected: it reads "new-born" out of the text).
--
-- AMBIGUITY: "unless the contrary is shown". What must be shown to the
-- contrary — the deemed birth facts of limb (a), the deemed parentage of
-- limb (b), or either? Reading (i): showing the contrary of EITHER deemed
-- fact defeats the deeming (taken here, as one rebuttal flag: subsection
-- (1) needs both limbs, so rebutting either defeats the s 1(1) route and a
-- single flag is extensionally adequate). Reading (ii): only a showing
-- against both limbs jointly rebuts (rejected: it would deem a child known
-- to have been born abroad to have been born here). Sergot et al. (1986)
-- §"Some Difficulties with the Formalization of Negation" (pp. 378-381)
-- wrestle with the same default; their encoding, like this one, makes
-- rebuttal an explicit input.

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(2); s 50(1) "commencement"
GIVEN person IS A PersonProfile
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`found abandoned in the United Kingdom after commencement` person MEANS
        person's `found abandoned as a new-born infant in the United Kingdom`
    AND person's `date found abandoned` AT LEAST `commencement`

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(2); s 50(1) "appointed day"
GIVEN person IS A PersonProfile
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`found abandoned in a qualifying territory on or after the appointed day` person MEANS
        person's `found abandoned as a new-born infant in a qualifying territory`
    AND person's `date found abandoned` AT LEAST `the appointed day`

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(2) — the abandonment limb
GIVEN person IS A PersonProfile
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`1(2) — found abandoned in the United Kingdom or a qualifying territory in time` person MEANS
        `found abandoned in the United Kingdom after commencement` person
    OR  `found abandoned in a qualifying territory on or after the appointed day` person

-- The deeming reaches ONLY subsection (1): "deemed for the purposes of
-- subsection (1)". A foundling gets no help toward subsection (1A) — the
-- deemed parent is "a British citizen or settled", not a member of the
-- armed forces.
@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(2)
GIVEN person IS A PersonProfile
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `deemed by subsection (2) to satisfy subsection (1)` person IF
        "(2)" ... `1(2) — found abandoned in the United Kingdom or a qualifying territory in time` person
    AND NOT person's `the contrary shown, rebutting the subsection (2) presumption`
    AND "(a) to have been born in the United Kingdom after commencement or in that territory on or after the appointed day; and"
    AND "(b) to have been born to a parent who at the time of the birth was a British citizen or settled in the United Kingdom or that territory."

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(1), (2)
GIVEN person IS A PersonProfile
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a British citizen by virtue of subsection (1) or (2)` person IF
        `1(1) — satisfied on the actual facts of the birth` person
    OR  `deemed by subsection (2) to satisfy subsection (1)` person

§§ `Subsection (1A) — the armed-forces route`

-- AMBIGUITY: "born in the United Kingdom or a qualifying territory on or
-- after the relevant day" — does "on or after the relevant day" qualify
-- both places of birth or only the qualifying-territory limb? Reading (i):
-- one date threshold governing both places (taken here: the subsection was
-- inserted whole in 2010 and a UK birth before the relevant day cannot have
-- an armed-forces route either). Reading (ii): the date attaches only to
-- territory births, UK births reaching back to commencement (rejected: it
-- would apply an inserted provision to two decades before its insertion).
@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(1A); s 50(1A)-(1B) construe "member of the armed forces"
GIVEN person IS A PersonProfile
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a British citizen by virtue of subsection (1A)` person IF
        "(1A)" ... `born in the United Kingdom or a qualifying territory` person
    AND person's `date of birth` AT LEAST `the relevant day`
    AND person's `father or mother a member of the armed forces at the time of the birth`

§§ `The subsection (3), (3A) and (4) chapeau — not already a citizen`

-- AMBIGUITY: "who is not a British citizen by virtue of subsection (1),
-- (1A) or (2) or section 10A". Subsection (2) confers nothing by itself —
-- it deems facts "for the purposes of subsection (1)" — so "by virtue of
-- ... (2)" must mean "by virtue of subsection (1) as read with (2)" (taken
-- here: the combined predicate below). Note also that the chapeau
-- enumerates ONLY these routes: a person who is a British citizen some
-- OTHER way (descent under s 2, registration under s 3, naturalisation) is
-- not excluded by the chapeau's words, and this encoding follows the words.
@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(3), (3A), (4) chapeau; s 10A
GIVEN person IS A PersonProfile
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`not already a British citizen by virtue of subsection (1), (1A) or (2) or section 10A` person MEANS
        NOT `a British citizen by virtue of subsection (1) or (2)` person
    AND NOT `a British citizen by virtue of subsection (1A)` person
    AND NOT person's `a British citizen under section 10A`

§§ `Subsection (3) — registration after a parent becomes a citizen or settled`

-- AMBIGUITY: "if, while he is a minor— (a) ... and (b) ..." — does the
-- chapeau's "while he is a minor" govern both limbs, or only limb (a)?
-- Reading (i): both the parent's change of status AND the application must
-- occur during minority (taken here — the temporal adverbial sits in the
-- chapeau and distributes over the enumerated list; both input fields are
-- worded "while a minor, ..."). Reading (ii): only limb (a) is
-- time-bounded and the application may come at any age (rejected: an adult
-- registrant has the s 4 routes, and the settled understanding requires an
-- in-minority application).
--
-- NOTE the asymmetry with subsection (1): subsections (3), (3A) and (4)
-- reach only births "in the United Kingdom" — a qualifying-territory birth
-- has no s 1 registration entitlement. That is the enacted text, not an
-- encoding shortcut.

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(3)(a)
GIVEN person IS A PersonProfile
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`1(3)(a) — father or mother becomes a British citizen or settled` person MEANS
        "(a)" ... person's `while a minor, father or mother became a British citizen or became settled in the United Kingdom`

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(3)(b)
GIVEN person IS A PersonProfile
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`1(3)(b) — an application is made while a minor` person MEANS
        "(b)" ... person's `while a minor, an application made for registration as a British citizen`

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(3)
GIVEN person IS A PersonProfile
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `entitled to be registered under subsection (3)` person IF
        "(3)" ... `born in the United Kingdom after commencement` person
    AND `not already a British citizen by virtue of subsection (1), (1A) or (2) or section 10A` person
    AND `1(3)(a) — father or mother becomes a British citizen or settled` person
    AND `1(3)(b) — an application is made while a minor` person

§§ `Subsection (3A) — registration after a parent joins the armed forces`

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(3A)(a)
GIVEN person IS A PersonProfile
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`1(3A)(a) — father or mother becomes a member of the armed forces` person MEANS
        "(a)" ... person's `while a minor, father or mother became a member of the armed forces`

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(3A)(b)
GIVEN person IS A PersonProfile
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`1(3A)(b) — an application is made while a minor` person MEANS
        "(b)" ... person's `while a minor, an application made for registration as a British citizen`

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(3A)
GIVEN person IS A PersonProfile
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `entitled to be registered under subsection (3A)` person IF
        "(3A)" ... `born in the United Kingdom on or after the relevant day` person
    AND `not already a British citizen by virtue of subsection (1), (1A) or (2) or section 10A` person
    AND `1(3A)(a) — father or mother becomes a member of the armed forces` person
    AND `1(3A)(b) — an application is made while a minor` person

§§ `Subsection (4) — registration on ten years' residence, and the subsection (7) dispensation`

-- AMBIGUITY: "as regards each of the first ten years of that person's
-- life" — years of LIFE (birthday to birthday) or calendar years? Reading
-- (i): the first ten years of life, measured from birth anniversary to
-- birth anniversary (taken here — "of that person's life" anchors the
-- years to the person, not the calendar). Reading (ii): the ten calendar
-- years containing the person's first decade (rejected: it makes the test
-- depend on the accident of a January or December birthday).
--
-- AMBIGUITY: counting "the number of days on which he was absent" — does a
-- day of departure or arrival, part-spent in the UK, count as a day of
-- absence? Reading (i): a day counts as absent only if the person was
-- outside the UK for the whole day (taken here as the input convention —
-- the NUMBER field carries whatever count the fact-supplier certifies).
-- Reading (ii): any day touched by absence counts (not ruled out by the
-- text; the burden sits on the fact-supplier, and the field is a single
-- per-year maximum: the statute's per-year universal "as regards each ...
-- does not exceed 90" is encoded extensionally as
-- max-over-the-ten-years <= 90, which is equivalent).

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(4) — the application limb
GIVEN person IS A PersonProfile
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`1(4) — application made after attaining the age of ten years` person MEANS
    person's `an application for registration made after attaining the age of ten years`

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(4) — the residence limb
GIVEN person IS A PersonProfile
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`1(4) — absences within the ninety-day ceiling in each of the first ten years` person MEANS
    person's `greatest number of days absent from the United Kingdom in any one of the first ten years of life` AT MOST `permitted days of absence in each of the first ten years`

-- Subsection (7) is encoded HERE, as the discretionary escape from the
-- subsection (4) residence limb. The EXERCISE of the discretion is an
-- input fact — whether the Secretary of State "thinks fit" is not a
-- computable question.
@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(7)
GIVEN person IS A PersonProfile
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`1(7) — the Secretary of State treats the residence requirement as fulfilled` person MEANS
        "(7)" ... person's `the Secretary of State treats the subsection (4) residence requirement as fulfilled`

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(4), (7)
GIVEN person IS A PersonProfile
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`1(4) — the residence requirement met or treated as met` person MEANS
        `1(4) — absences within the ninety-day ceiling in each of the first ten years` person
    OR  `1(7) — the Secretary of State treats the residence requirement as fulfilled` person

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(4)
GIVEN person IS A PersonProfile
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `entitled to be registered under subsection (4)` person IF
        "(4)" ... `born in the United Kingdom after commencement` person
    AND `not already a British citizen by virtue of subsection (1), (1A) or (2) or section 10A` person
    AND `1(4) — application made after attaining the age of ten years` person
    AND `1(4) — the residence requirement met or treated as met` person

§§ `The good-character restriction on granting registration (modification C3)`

-- NOT part of the s 1 text. Modification note C3: s 1(3)/(4) registration
-- was restricted (4.12.2006) by Immigration, Asylum and Nationality Act
-- 2006 s 58 — the good-character requirement now embodied in s 41A of this
-- Act, which applies to applications under s 1(3), (3A) or (4) by persons
-- aged 10 or over at the date of application.
--
-- AMBIGUITY: (of encoding placement, not of the words) does the
-- restriction narrow the s 1 ENTITLEMENT, or only the GRANT of the
-- application? Reading (i): the entitlement subsists as s 1 states it, and
-- s 41A separately forbids granting the application to a person not of
-- good character (taken here — the restriction is drafted against the
-- registration provisions, not woven into s 1, so it is encoded as this
-- separate grant-gate and the s 1 predicates above stay pure). Reading
-- (ii): fold good character into each entitlement predicate (rejected: it
-- would misstate what s 1 itself says, and the s 41A text was not fetched
-- verbatim so it may not ride inline as inert prose).
@ref Immigration, Asylum and Nationality Act 2006 s 58; British Nationality Act 1981 s 41A — modification C3, not part of the s 1 text
GIVEN person IS A PersonProfile
      `entitled under subsection (3), (3A) or (4)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`registration may be granted` person `entitled under subsection (3), (3A) or (4)` MEANS
        `entitled under subsection (3), (3A) or (4)`
    AND person's `of good character, where the good character requirement applies`

§§ `Subsections (5), (5A) and (6) — acquisition by adoption`

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(5), (5A), (6)
DECLARE AdoptionCase HAS
    `the adopted person a minor on the date of the order or adoption`                  IS A BOOLEAN
    `the adopted person already a British citizen`                                     IS A BOOLEAN
    `an order of a court in the United Kingdom authorising the adoption`               IS A BOOLEAN
    `an order of a court in a qualifying territory authorising the adoption`           IS A BOOLEAN
    `a Convention adoption effected under the law of a country or territory outside the United Kingdom`    IS A BOOLEAN
    `date of the order or Convention adoption`                                         IS A DATE
    `the adopter or, in a joint adoption, one of the adopters a British citizen on that date`              IS A BOOLEAN
    `the adopter or, in a joint adoption, both of the adopters habitually resident in the United Kingdom or a designated territory on that date` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the order or Convention adoption since ceased to have effect, whether on annulment or otherwise`      IS A BOOLEAN

-- AMBIGUITY: in s 1(5)(a) the qualifying-territory words read "or, on or
-- after the appointed day, any court in a qualifying territory makes an
-- order" — the date qualifies the MAKING of the order, so a
-- qualifying-territory order made before 21 May 2002 does not engage limb
-- (a). Reading taken: order date >= the appointed day for territory
-- courts, no date condition for United Kingdom courts (their competence
-- runs from the 2003 substitution of the whole subsection, which is the
-- text in force and the only text encoded).
@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(5)(a)
GIVEN adoption IS AN AdoptionCase
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`order of a court in a qualifying territory on or after the appointed day` adoption MEANS
        adoption's `an order of a court in a qualifying territory authorising the adoption`
    AND adoption's `date of the order or Convention adoption` AT LEAST `the appointed day`

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(5)(a)
GIVEN adoption IS AN AdoptionCase
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`1(5)(a) — a court order authorising the adoption` adoption MEANS
        "(a)"
    ..  adoption's `an order of a court in the United Kingdom authorising the adoption`
    ..  `order of a court in a qualifying territory on or after the appointed day` adoption

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(5)(b); s 50(1) "Convention adoption"
GIVEN adoption IS AN AdoptionCase
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`1(5)(b) — adopted under a Convention adoption` adoption MEANS
        "(b)" ... adoption's `a Convention adoption effected under the law of a country or territory outside the United Kingdom`

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(5)(a)-(b)
GIVEN adoption IS AN AdoptionCase
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`1(5) — an order is made or a Convention adoption is effected` adoption MEANS
        `1(5)(a) — a court order authorising the adoption` adoption
    OR  `1(5)(b) — adopted under a Convention adoption` adoption

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(5) — "a minor who is not a British citizen"
GIVEN adoption IS AN AdoptionCase
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the adopted person a minor and not already a British citizen` adoption MEANS
        adoption's `the adopted person a minor on the date of the order or adoption`
    AND NOT adoption's `the adopted person already a British citizen`

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(5A)(a)
GIVEN adoption IS AN AdoptionCase
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`1(5A)(a) — the adopter, or one joint adopter, a British citizen` adoption MEANS
        "(a)" ... adoption's `the adopter or, in a joint adoption, one of the adopters a British citizen on that date`

-- AMBIGUITY: "(b) ... both of the adopters are habitually resident in the
-- United Kingdom or in a designated territory" — must joint adopters be
-- habitually resident in the SAME place, or does each independently
-- satisfy "in the United Kingdom or in a designated territory"? Reading
-- (i): each adopter habitually resident somewhere within the listed places
-- (taken here — the disjunction distributes over the adopters, and the
-- input field carries the statutory phrase whole). Reading (ii): both in
-- one and the same place (not compelled by the words; nothing in the
-- encoding turns on it because the field arrives pre-construed).
-- The proviso shape: limb (b) binds only "in a case within subsection
-- (5)(b)" — a material implication, vacuously satisfied for domestic and
-- territory court orders. Drafting pattern: NOT X .. Y.
@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(5A)(b); s 50(1) "designated territory"
GIVEN adoption IS AN AdoptionCase
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`1(5A)(b) — habitual residence, in a Convention case` adoption MEANS
        "(b)"
    ..  NOT adoption's `a Convention adoption effected under the law of a country or territory outside the United Kingdom`
    ..  adoption's `the adopter or, in a joint adoption, both of the adopters habitually resident in the United Kingdom or a designated territory on that date`

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(5A)
GIVEN adoption IS AN AdoptionCase
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`1(5A) — the requirements are met on the date of the order or adoption` adoption MEANS
        "(5A) Those requirements are that on the date on which the order is made or the Convention adoption is effected (as the case may be)—"
    ... `1(5A)(a) — the adopter, or one joint adopter, a British citizen` adoption
    AND `1(5A)(b) — habitual residence, in a Convention case` adoption

@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(5)
GIVEN adoption IS AN AdoptionCase
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a British citizen by virtue of subsection (5)` adoption IF
    `the adopted person a minor and not already a British citizen` adoption
    AND `1(5) — an order is made or a Convention adoption is effected` adoption
    AND `1(5A) — the requirements are met on the date of the order or adoption` adoption

-- Subsection (6) is an INVARIANCE: citizenship acquired under subsection
-- (5) survives the order or Convention adoption later ceasing to have
-- effect. Encoded by deliberately NOT reading the cesser field — the
-- predicate below equals `a British citizen by virtue of subsection (5)`
-- whatever the cesser flag says, and the tests assert exactly that.
@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(6)
GIVEN adoption IS AN AdoptionCase
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a British citizen notwithstanding cesser of the order — subsection (6)` adoption IF
        "(6)" ... `a British citizen by virtue of subsection (5)` adoption

§§ `The section 1 outcome at birth, as one total answer`

DECLARE BirthOutcome IS ONE OF
    `a British citizen from birth under subsection (1)`
    `a British citizen from birth under subsection (1A)`
    `not a British citizen from birth under section 1`

-- The routes overlap (a settled parent may also serve in the armed
-- forces); the order below is presentational priority, not statutory
-- hierarchy — subsection (1) is reported first because it is the older and
-- more general route.
@ref British Nationality Act 1981 s 1(1)-(2), (1A)
GIVEN person IS A PersonProfile
GIVETH A BirthOutcome
`section 1 outcome at birth` person MEANS
    BRANCH IF `a British citizen by virtue of subsection (1) or (2)` person THEN `a British citizen from birth under subsection (1)`
           IF `a British citizen by virtue of subsection (1A)` person       THEN `a British citizen from birth under subsection (1A)`
           OTHERWISE `not a British citizen from birth under section 1`

§ `Tests`

§§ `Fixtures`

-- The classic example lineage of Sergot, Sadri, Kowalski, Kriwaczek,
-- Hammond & Cory, "The British Nationality Act as a Logic Program", CACM
-- 29(5) 370-386 (1986). The paper's PROLOG section (p. 372) supplies
-- "Peter was born in the U.K." with William his father; Figure 4, the
-- APES dialogue extract (pp. 376-377), dates the birth 3 May 1983 and has
-- William "a citizen of the U.K. and Colonies on date (31 Dec 1982)" with
-- the right of abode — a British citizen at commencement (s 11(1)), hence
-- at the birth. Adapted to the current consolidated text. Exercises
-- s 1(1)(a).
`Peter` MEANS PersonProfile WITH
    name                                              IS "Peter"
    `born in the United Kingdom`                      IS TRUE
    `born in a qualifying territory`                  IS FALSE
    `date of birth`                                   IS YMD 1983 5 3
    `father or mother a British citizen at the time of the birth`                                          IS TRUE
    `father or mother settled in the United Kingdom or the territory of birth at the time of the birth`    IS FALSE
    `father or mother a member of the armed forces at the time of the birth`                               IS FALSE
    `found abandoned as a new-born infant in the United Kingdom`                                           IS FALSE
    `found abandoned as a new-born infant in a qualifying territory`                                       IS FALSE
    `date found abandoned`                            IS YMD 1983 5 3
    `the contrary shown, rebutting the subsection (2) presumption`                                         IS FALSE
    `a British citizen under section 10A`             IS FALSE
    `while a minor, father or mother became a British citizen or became settled in the United Kingdom`     IS FALSE
    `while a minor, father or mother became a member of the armed forces`                                  IS FALSE
    `while a minor, an application made for registration as a British citizen`                             IS FALSE
    `an application for registration made after attaining the age of ten years`                            IS FALSE
    `greatest number of days absent from the United Kingdom in any one of the first ten years of life`     IS 0
    `the Secretary of State treats the subsection (4) residence requirement as fulfilled`                  IS FALSE
    `of good character, where the good character requirement applies`                                      IS TRUE

-- Peter again, with both subsection (3) limbs deliberately satisfied — the
-- parent later becomes settled AND an application is made while he is a
-- minor — so that the Group 4 negative on this fixture is carried by the
-- "not already a British citizen" chapeau ALONE.
`Peter with the subsection (3) limbs satisfied` MEANS PersonProfile WITH
    name                                              IS "Peter"
    `born in the United Kingdom`                      IS TRUE
    `born in a qualifying territory`                  IS FALSE
    `date of birth`                                   IS YMD 1983 5 3
    `father or mother a British citizen at the time of the birth`                                          IS TRUE
    `father or mother settled in the United Kingdom or the territory of birth at the time of the birth`    IS FALSE
    `father or mother a member of the armed forces at the time of the birth`                               IS FALSE
    `found abandoned as a new-born infant in the United Kingdom`                                           IS FALSE
    `found abandoned as a new-born infant in a qualifying territory`                                       IS FALSE
    `date found abandoned`                            IS YMD 1983 5 3
    `the contrary shown, rebutting the subsection (2) presumption`                                         IS FALSE
    `a British citizen under section 10A`             IS FALSE
    `while a minor, father or mother became a British citizen or became settled in the United Kingdom`     IS TRUE
    `while a minor, father or mother became a member of the armed forces`                                  IS FALSE
    `while a minor, an application made for registration as a British citizen`                             IS TRUE
    `an application for registration made after attaining the age of ten years`                            IS FALSE
    `greatest number of days absent from the United Kingdom in any one of the first ten years of life`     IS 0
    `the Secretary of State treats the subsection (4) residence requirement as fulfilled`                  IS FALSE
    `of good character, where the good character requirement applies`                                      IS TRUE

-- Boundary-sweep constructor for birth cases: a child born in the United
-- Kingdom, varying the birth date and the three parent statuses.
GIVEN dob              IS A DATE
      `citizen parent` IS A BOOLEAN
      `settled parent` IS A BOOLEAN
      `forces parent`  IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A PersonProfile
`a child born in the United Kingdom on` dob `citizen parent` `settled parent` `forces parent` MEANS PersonProfile WITH
    name                                              IS "a UK-born child"
    `born in the United Kingdom`                      IS TRUE
    `born in a qualifying territory`                  IS FALSE
    `date of birth`                                   IS dob
    `father or mother a British citizen at the time of the birth`                                          IS `citizen parent`
    `father or mother settled in the United Kingdom or the territory of birth at the time of the birth`    IS `settled parent`
    `father or mother a member of the armed forces at the time of the birth`                               IS `forces parent`
    `found abandoned as a new-born infant in the United Kingdom`                                           IS FALSE
    `found abandoned as a new-born infant in a qualifying territory`                                       IS FALSE
    `date found abandoned`                            IS dob
    `the contrary shown, rebutting the subsection (2) presumption`                                         IS FALSE
    `a British citizen under section 10A`             IS FALSE
    `while a minor, father or mother became a British citizen or became settled in the United Kingdom`     IS FALSE
    `while a minor, father or mother became a member of the armed forces`                                  IS FALSE
    `while a minor, an application made for registration as a British citizen`                             IS FALSE
    `an application for registration made after attaining the age of ten years`                            IS FALSE
    `greatest number of days absent from the United Kingdom in any one of the first ten years of life`     IS 0
    `the Secretary of State treats the subsection (4) residence requirement as fulfilled`                  IS FALSE
    `of good character, where the good character requirement applies`                                      IS TRUE

-- The same, born in a qualifying territory (e.g. Bermuda, Gibraltar).
GIVEN dob              IS A DATE
      `citizen parent` IS A BOOLEAN
      `settled parent` IS A BOOLEAN
      `forces parent`  IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A PersonProfile
`a child born in a qualifying territory on` dob `citizen parent` `settled parent` `forces parent` MEANS PersonProfile WITH
    name                                              IS "a territory-born child"
    `born in the United Kingdom`                      IS FALSE
    `born in a qualifying territory`                  IS TRUE
    `date of birth`                                   IS dob
    `father or mother a British citizen at the time of the birth`                                          IS `citizen parent`
    `father or mother settled in the United Kingdom or the territory of birth at the time of the birth`    IS `settled parent`
    `father or mother a member of the armed forces at the time of the birth`                               IS `forces parent`
    `found abandoned as a new-born infant in the United Kingdom`                                           IS FALSE
    `found abandoned as a new-born infant in a qualifying territory`                                       IS FALSE
    `date found abandoned`                            IS dob
    `the contrary shown, rebutting the subsection (2) presumption`                                         IS FALSE
    `a British citizen under section 10A`             IS FALSE
    `while a minor, father or mother became a British citizen or became settled in the United Kingdom`     IS FALSE
    `while a minor, father or mother became a member of the armed forces`                                  IS FALSE
    `while a minor, an application made for registration as a British citizen`                             IS FALSE
    `an application for registration made after attaining the age of ten years`                            IS FALSE
    `greatest number of days absent from the United Kingdom in any one of the first ten years of life`     IS 0
    `the Secretary of State treats the subsection (4) residence requirement as fulfilled`                  IS FALSE
    `of good character, where the good character requirement applies`                                      IS TRUE

-- A foundling: place and date of birth, and parentage, are unknown — the
-- subsection (2) deeming supplies them. `date of birth` is set to the
-- found date only because the record requires a value; no subsection (2)
-- path reads it. `in the United Kingdom` FALSE means found in a
-- qualifying territory instead.
GIVEN `found date`            IS A DATE
      `in the United Kingdom` IS A BOOLEAN
      `contrary shown`        IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A PersonProfile
`a foundling found on` `found date` `in the United Kingdom` `contrary shown` MEANS PersonProfile WITH
    name                                              IS "a foundling"
    `born in the United Kingdom`                      IS FALSE
    `born in a qualifying territory`                  IS FALSE
    `date of birth`                                   IS `found date`
    `father or mother a British citizen at the time of the birth`                                          IS FALSE
    `father or mother settled in the United Kingdom or the territory of birth at the time of the birth`    IS FALSE
    `father or mother a member of the armed forces at the time of the birth`                               IS FALSE
    `found abandoned as a new-born infant in the United Kingdom`                                           IS `in the United Kingdom`
    `found abandoned as a new-born infant in a qualifying territory`                                       IS NOT `in the United Kingdom`
    `date found abandoned`                            IS `found date`
    `the contrary shown, rebutting the subsection (2) presumption`                                         IS `contrary shown`
    `a British citizen under section 10A`             IS FALSE
    `while a minor, father or mother became a British citizen or became settled in the United Kingdom`     IS FALSE
    `while a minor, father or mother became a member of the armed forces`                                  IS FALSE
    `while a minor, an application made for registration as a British citizen`                             IS FALSE
    `an application for registration made after attaining the age of ten years`                            IS FALSE
    `greatest number of days absent from the United Kingdom in any one of the first ten years of life`     IS 0
    `the Secretary of State treats the subsection (4) residence requirement as fulfilled`                  IS FALSE
    `of good character, where the good character requirement applies`                                      IS TRUE

-- A child born in the United Kingdom to parents with no qualifying status
-- at the birth (visitors, students, unsettled migrants), varying the
-- later-life events that drive subsections (3) and (3A).
GIVEN dob                                    IS A DATE
      `parent later a citizen or settled`    IS A BOOLEAN
      `parent later in the forces`           IS A BOOLEAN
      `application while a minor`            IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A PersonProfile
`a child of visitors born on` dob `parent later a citizen or settled` `parent later in the forces` `application while a minor` MEANS PersonProfile WITH
    name                                              IS "a child of visitors"
    `born in the United Kingdom`                      IS TRUE
    `born in a qualifying territory`                  IS FALSE
    `date of birth`                                   IS dob
    `father or mother a British citizen at the time of the birth`                                          IS FALSE
    `father or mother settled in the United Kingdom or the territory of birth at the time of the birth`    IS FALSE
    `father or mother a member of the armed forces at the time of the birth`                               IS FALSE
    `found abandoned as a new-born infant in the United Kingdom`                                           IS FALSE
    `found abandoned as a new-born infant in a qualifying territory`                                       IS FALSE
    `date found abandoned`                            IS dob
    `the contrary shown, rebutting the subsection (2) presumption`                                         IS FALSE
    `a British citizen under section 10A`             IS FALSE
    `while a minor, father or mother became a British citizen or became settled in the United Kingdom`     IS `parent later a citizen or settled`
    `while a minor, father or mother became a member of the armed forces`                                  IS `parent later in the forces`
    `while a minor, an application made for registration as a British citizen`                             IS `application while a minor`
    `an application for registration made after attaining the age of ten years`                            IS FALSE
    `greatest number of days absent from the United Kingdom in any one of the first ten years of life`     IS 0
    `the Secretary of State treats the subsection (4) residence requirement as fulfilled`                  IS FALSE
    `of good character, where the good character requirement applies`                                      IS TRUE

-- The subsection (4) family: UK-born to non-qualifying parents, varying
-- the residence record, the over-ten application, the subsection (7)
-- dispensation, and good character.
GIVEN dob                     IS A DATE
      `days absent`           IS A NUMBER
      `application after ten` IS A BOOLEAN
      `waiver`                IS A BOOLEAN
      `good character`        IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A PersonProfile
`a ten-year applicant born on` dob `days absent` `application after ten` `waiver` `good character` MEANS PersonProfile WITH
    name                                              IS "a ten-year applicant"
    `born in the United Kingdom`                      IS TRUE
    `born in a qualifying territory`                  IS FALSE
    `date of birth`                                   IS dob
    `father or mother a British citizen at the time of the birth`                                          IS FALSE
    `father or mother settled in the United Kingdom or the territory of birth at the time of the birth`    IS FALSE
    `father or mother a member of the armed forces at the time of the birth`                               IS FALSE
    `found abandoned as a new-born infant in the United Kingdom`                                           IS FALSE
    `found abandoned as a new-born infant in a qualifying territory`                                       IS FALSE
    `date found abandoned`                            IS dob
    `the contrary shown, rebutting the subsection (2) presumption`                                         IS FALSE
    `a British citizen under section 10A`             IS FALSE
    `while a minor, father or mother became a British citizen or became settled in the United Kingdom`     IS FALSE
    `while a minor, father or mother became a member of the armed forces`                                  IS FALSE
    `while a minor, an application made for registration as a British citizen`                             IS FALSE
    `an application for registration made after attaining the age of ten years`                            IS `application after ten`
    `greatest number of days absent from the United Kingdom in any one of the first ten years of life`     IS `days absent`
    `the Secretary of State treats the subsection (4) residence requirement as fulfilled`                  IS `waiver`
    `of good character, where the good character requirement applies`                                      IS `good character`

GIVEN `order date`          IS A DATE
      `adopter a citizen`   IS A BOOLEAN
      `habitually resident` IS A BOOLEAN
      `later ceased`        IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN AdoptionCase
`a United Kingdom adoption order on` `order date` `adopter a citizen` `habitually resident` `later ceased` MEANS AdoptionCase WITH
    `the adopted person a minor on the date of the order or adoption`                  IS TRUE
    `the adopted person already a British citizen`                                     IS FALSE
    `an order of a court in the United Kingdom authorising the adoption`               IS TRUE
    `an order of a court in a qualifying territory authorising the adoption`           IS FALSE
    `a Convention adoption effected under the law of a country or territory outside the United Kingdom`    IS FALSE
    `date of the order or Convention adoption`                                         IS `order date`
    `the adopter or, in a joint adoption, one of the adopters a British citizen on that date`              IS `adopter a citizen`
    `the adopter or, in a joint adoption, both of the adopters habitually resident in the United Kingdom or a designated territory on that date` IS `habitually resident`
    `the order or Convention adoption since ceased to have effect, whether on annulment or otherwise`      IS `later ceased`

GIVEN `adoption date`       IS A DATE
      `adopter a citizen`   IS A BOOLEAN
      `habitually resident` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN AdoptionCase
`a Convention adoption on` `adoption date` `adopter a citizen` `habitually resident` MEANS AdoptionCase WITH
    `the adopted person a minor on the date of the order or adoption`                  IS TRUE
    `the adopted person already a British citizen`                                     IS FALSE
    `an order of a court in the United Kingdom authorising the adoption`               IS FALSE
    `an order of a court in a qualifying territory authorising the adoption`           IS FALSE
    `a Convention adoption effected under the law of a country or territory outside the United Kingdom`    IS TRUE
    `date of the order or Convention adoption`                                         IS `adoption date`
    `the adopter or, in a joint adoption, one of the adopters a British citizen on that date`              IS `adopter a citizen`
    `the adopter or, in a joint adoption, both of the adopters habitually resident in the United Kingdom or a designated territory on that date` IS `habitually resident`
    `the order or Convention adoption since ceased to have effect, whether on annulment or otherwise`      IS FALSE

-- Exercises the qualifying-territory court limb of (5)(a), AND the (5A)(b)
-- proviso: the adopters are NOT habitually resident in the UK or a
-- designated territory, but (5A)(b) binds only in Convention cases, so the
-- adoption still confers citizenship.
`a Gibraltar adoption order case` MEANS AdoptionCase WITH
    `the adopted person a minor on the date of the order or adoption`                  IS TRUE
    `the adopted person already a British citizen`                                     IS FALSE
    `an order of a court in the United Kingdom authorising the adoption`               IS FALSE
    `an order of a court in a qualifying territory authorising the adoption`           IS TRUE
    `a Convention adoption effected under the law of a country or territory outside the United Kingdom`    IS FALSE
    `date of the order or Convention adoption`                                         IS YMD 2005 4 1
    `the adopter or, in a joint adoption, one of the adopters a British citizen on that date`              IS TRUE
    `the adopter or, in a joint adoption, both of the adopters habitually resident in the United Kingdom or a designated territory on that date` IS FALSE
    `the order or Convention adoption since ceased to have effect, whether on annulment or otherwise`      IS FALSE

-- The Gibraltar case with the order made before the appointed day: the
-- qualifying-territory limb of (5)(a) is not engaged.
`a pre-appointed-day Gibraltar order case` MEANS AdoptionCase WITH
    `the adopted person a minor on the date of the order or adoption`                  IS TRUE
    `the adopted person already a British citizen`                                     IS FALSE
    `an order of a court in the United Kingdom authorising the adoption`               IS FALSE
    `an order of a court in a qualifying territory authorising the adoption`           IS TRUE
    `a Convention adoption effected under the law of a country or territory outside the United Kingdom`    IS FALSE
    `date of the order or Convention adoption`                                         IS YMD 2001 4 1
    `the adopter or, in a joint adoption, one of the adopters a British citizen on that date`              IS TRUE
    `the adopter or, in a joint adoption, both of the adopters habitually resident in the United Kingdom or a designated territory on that date` IS FALSE
    `the order or Convention adoption since ceased to have effect, whether on annulment or otherwise`      IS FALSE

-- The chapeau negative: subsection (5) reaches only "a minor who is not a
-- British citizen".
`an adoption of a person already a British citizen` MEANS AdoptionCase WITH
    `the adopted person a minor on the date of the order or adoption`                  IS TRUE
    `the adopted person already a British citizen`                                     IS TRUE
    `an order of a court in the United Kingdom authorising the adoption`               IS TRUE
    `an order of a court in a qualifying territory authorising the adoption`           IS FALSE
    `a Convention adoption effected under the law of a country or territory outside the United Kingdom`    IS FALSE
    `date of the order or Convention adoption`                                         IS YMD 2010 9 15
    `the adopter or, in a joint adoption, one of the adopters a British citizen on that date`              IS TRUE
    `the adopter or, in a joint adoption, both of the adopters habitually resident in the United Kingdom or a designated territory on that date` IS TRUE
    `the order or Convention adoption since ceased to have effect, whether on annulment or otherwise`      IS FALSE

-- Named lead cases.
`the foundling`           MEANS `a foundling found on` (YMD 2020 6 1) TRUE FALSE
`the rebutted foundling`  MEANS `a foundling found on` (YMD 2020 6 1) TRUE TRUE
`the forces child`        MEANS `a child born in the United Kingdom on` (YMD 2015 6 1) FALSE FALSE TRUE
`the child of visitors`   MEANS `a child of visitors born on` (YMD 2012 5 10) TRUE FALSE TRUE
`the ten-year lead case`  MEANS `a ten-year applicant born on` (YMD 1995 3 1) 90 TRUE FALSE TRUE
`the applicant of bad character` MEANS `a ten-year applicant born on` (YMD 1995 3 1) 90 TRUE FALSE FALSE

§§ `Group 1 — subsection (1), boundaries at commencement and the appointed day`

-- Sergot et al.'s Peter, on the current text: born in the UK in 1983 to a
-- British-citizen parent.
#ASSERT     `a British citizen by virtue of subsection (1) or (2)` `Peter`
-- Born on commencement day itself, to a settled (not citizen) parent:
-- limb (b), and the "after commencement" boundary.
#ASSERT     `a British citizen by virtue of subsection (1) or (2)` (`a child born in the United Kingdom on` (YMD 1983 1 1) FALSE TRUE FALSE)
-- One day earlier: the 1981 Act confers nothing (the 1948 Act governed).
#ASSERT NOT `a British citizen by virtue of subsection (1) or (2)` (`a child born in the United Kingdom on` (YMD 1982 12 31) TRUE FALSE FALSE)
-- Born in the UK to parents with no qualifying status: not a citizen at birth.
#ASSERT NOT `a British citizen by virtue of subsection (1) or (2)` `the child of visitors`
-- Born in a qualifying territory ON the appointed day, settled parent: in.
#ASSERT     `a British citizen by virtue of subsection (1) or (2)` (`a child born in a qualifying territory on` (YMD 2002 5 21) FALSE TRUE FALSE)
-- One day before the appointed day: out.
#ASSERT NOT `a British citizen by virtue of subsection (1) or (2)` (`a child born in a qualifying territory on` (YMD 2002 5 20) FALSE TRUE FALSE)

§§ `Group 2 — subsection (1A), boundary at the relevant day (13 January 2010)`

-- Born in the UK ON the relevant day to an armed-forces parent: in.
#ASSERT     `a British citizen by virtue of subsection (1A)` (`a child born in the United Kingdom on` (YMD 2010 1 13) FALSE FALSE TRUE)
-- One day earlier: out.
#ASSERT NOT `a British citizen by virtue of subsection (1A)` (`a child born in the United Kingdom on` (YMD 2010 1 12) FALSE FALSE TRUE)
-- The route also runs in a qualifying territory.
#ASSERT     `a British citizen by virtue of subsection (1A)` (`a child born in a qualifying territory on` (YMD 2012 3 1) FALSE FALSE TRUE)
#ASSERT     `a British citizen by virtue of subsection (1A)` `the forces child`
-- No armed-forces parent: the limb fails.
#ASSERT NOT `a British citizen by virtue of subsection (1A)` (`a child born in the United Kingdom on` (YMD 2015 6 1) FALSE FALSE FALSE)

§§ `Group 3 — subsection (2), the foundling presumption`

#ASSERT     `deemed by subsection (2) to satisfy subsection (1)` `the foundling`
-- The deeming feeds subsection (1): the foundling is a citizen at birth.
#ASSERT     `a British citizen by virtue of subsection (1) or (2)` `the foundling`
-- The presumption is rebuttable: the contrary shown, the deeming fails.
#ASSERT NOT `a British citizen by virtue of subsection (1) or (2)` `the rebutted foundling`
-- Found before commencement: the deeming does not run.
#ASSERT NOT `deemed by subsection (2) to satisfy subsection (1)` (`a foundling found on` (YMD 1982 6 1) TRUE FALSE)

§§ `Group 4 — subsection (3), registration after a parent qualifies`

#ASSERT     `entitled to be registered under subsection (3)` `the child of visitors`
-- No application made while a minor: limb (b) fails.
#ASSERT NOT `entitled to be registered under subsection (3)` (`a child of visitors born on` (YMD 2012 5 10) TRUE FALSE FALSE)
-- The parent never becomes a citizen or settled: limb (a) fails.
#ASSERT NOT `entitled to be registered under subsection (3)` (`a child of visitors born on` (YMD 2012 5 10) FALSE FALSE TRUE)
-- Peter is already a British citizen by virtue of subsection (1). On plain
-- Peter this negative is overdetermined — limbs (a) and (b) fail on his
-- facts too — so by itself it does not demonstrate the chapeau.
#ASSERT NOT `entitled to be registered under subsection (3)` `Peter`
-- The chapeau in isolation: both while-a-minor limbs satisfied, and the
-- entitlement still fails, solely because Peter is already a British
-- citizen by virtue of subsection (1).
#ASSERT NOT `entitled to be registered under subsection (3)` `Peter with the subsection (3) limbs satisfied`

§§ `Group 5 — subsection (3A), registration after a parent joins the forces`

#ASSERT     `entitled to be registered under subsection (3A)` (`a child of visitors born on` (YMD 2012 5 10) FALSE TRUE TRUE)
-- Born before the relevant day: the (3A) chapeau fails (though (3) may
-- still serve such a person on its own facts).
#ASSERT NOT `entitled to be registered under subsection (3A)` (`a child of visitors born on` (YMD 2005 5 10) FALSE TRUE TRUE)
-- No application made while a minor: limb (b) fails.
#ASSERT NOT `entitled to be registered under subsection (3A)` (`a child of visitors born on` (YMD 2012 5 10) FALSE TRUE FALSE)

§§ `Group 6 — subsection (4) with (7), boundary at 90 days; and the C3 grant gate`

-- Exactly 90 days absent in the worst year: within the ceiling.
#ASSERT     `entitled to be registered under subsection (4)` `the ten-year lead case`
-- One day more: outside.
#ASSERT NOT `entitled to be registered under subsection (4)` (`a ten-year applicant born on` (YMD 1995 3 1) 91 TRUE FALSE TRUE)
-- The same excess, but the Secretary of State exercises the subsection (7)
-- dispensation: entitled.
#ASSERT     `entitled to be registered under subsection (4)` (`a ten-year applicant born on` (YMD 1995 3 1) 91 TRUE TRUE TRUE)
-- No application made after attaining ten: the application limb fails.
#ASSERT NOT `entitled to be registered under subsection (4)` (`a ten-year applicant born on` (YMD 1995 3 1) 90 FALSE FALSE TRUE)
-- The s 41A good-character gate (modification C3) sits on the GRANT, not
-- the entitlement: the applicant of bad character remains entitled under
-- s 1(4), but registration may not be granted.
#ASSERT     `entitled to be registered under subsection (4)` `the applicant of bad character`
#ASSERT     `registration may be granted` `the ten-year lead case` (`entitled to be registered under subsection (4)` `the ten-year lead case`)
#ASSERT NOT `registration may be granted` `the applicant of bad character` (`entitled to be registered under subsection (4)` `the applicant of bad character`)

§§ `Group 7 — subsections (5), (5A) and (6), acquisition by adoption`

-- A United Kingdom adoption order, adopter a British citizen: citizen from
-- the date of the order.
#ASSERT     `a British citizen by virtue of subsection (5)` (`a United Kingdom adoption order on` (YMD 2004 7 1) TRUE TRUE FALSE)
-- Adopter not a British citizen: (5A)(a) fails.
#ASSERT NOT `a British citizen by virtue of subsection (5)` (`a United Kingdom adoption order on` (YMD 2004 7 1) FALSE TRUE FALSE)
-- A Convention adoption, adopter a citizen and habitually resident: in.
#ASSERT     `a British citizen by virtue of subsection (5)` (`a Convention adoption on` (YMD 2006 2 1) TRUE TRUE)
-- A Convention adoption where the adopters are not habitually resident in
-- the UK or a designated territory: (5A)(b) fails.
#ASSERT NOT `a British citizen by virtue of subsection (5)` (`a Convention adoption on` (YMD 2006 2 1) TRUE FALSE)
-- A qualifying-territory court order: (5A)(b) does not bind outside
-- Convention cases, so non-residence does not matter.
#ASSERT     `a British citizen by virtue of subsection (5)` `a Gibraltar adoption order case`
-- A qualifying-territory court order made BEFORE the appointed day does not
-- engage limb (5)(a) — only the timing differs from the Gibraltar case.
#ASSERT NOT `1(5)(a) — a court order authorising the adoption` `a pre-appointed-day Gibraltar order case`
-- Subsection (5) reaches only a minor who is NOT already a British citizen.
#ASSERT NOT `a British citizen by virtue of subsection (5)` `an adoption of a person already a British citizen`
-- Subsection (6): annulment of the order does not disturb the status.
#ASSERT     `a British citizen notwithstanding cesser of the order — subsection (6)` (`a United Kingdom adoption order on` (YMD 2004 7 1) TRUE TRUE TRUE)
-- But cesser cannot resurrect a citizenship that never arose.
#ASSERT NOT `a British citizen notwithstanding cesser of the order — subsection (6)` (`a United Kingdom adoption order on` (YMD 2004 7 1) FALSE TRUE TRUE)

§§ `Group 8 — the total outcome`

#EVAL `section 1 outcome at birth` `Peter`

#ASSERT `section 1 outcome at birth` `Peter` EQUALS `a British citizen from birth under subsection (1)`
#ASSERT `section 1 outcome at birth` `the forces child` EQUALS `a British citizen from birth under subsection (1A)`
#ASSERT `section 1 outcome at birth` `the child of visitors` EQUALS `not a British citizen from birth under section 1`
